es/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(4)) &= f^{-1}(-1) \\\\\n4 &= f^{-1}(-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082905 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082905 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082905 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(-4)) &= f^{-1}(3) \\\\\n- 4 &= f^{-1}(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(3) = -4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082906 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082906 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082906 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082906 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(2)) &= f^{-1}(-3) \\\\\n2 &= f^{-1}(-3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-3) = 2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082907 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082907 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then what is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082907 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "062748b1-c85a-5c2c-8d1f-f1006e73a880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082907 #edb/ref 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 4x - 2k \\\\\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k \\\\\n&= 4(\\frac{x}{4} - 2) - 2k \\\\\n&= x - 8 - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f$, we also know that\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nEquating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\n- 8 - 2k &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= -8 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083356 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" #edb/ref 1759218604